          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong canonical formula of mythical transformation
            is an expression proposed in 1955 by the anthropologist
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claude+L%C3%A9vi-Strauss"} Lévi-Strauss
            in order to account for the abstract relations occuring between characters and their attributes in a myth understood as the collection of its variants. The vagueness of the initial proposal has been lifted by several mathematical interpretations in the aftermath.
          %h2#the_original_formulation The original formulation
          %p
            In Lévi-Strauss (
            %a{:href => "#LS55"}> 1955
            , p.442) the canonical formula is introduced as the “algebraic” expression:
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_x(a): f_y(b)\cong f_x(b):f_{a^{-1}}(y)
            \.
